  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Response to Cyclone Mocha: Rehabilitation of Camp Structures, through Strengthened Site Development and Site Management to Improve Rohingya Refugees&#x2019; Living Conditions</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cox&#x2019;s Bazar district in Bangladesh, and the Rohingya refugee camps located there, are in one of the most disaster-vulnerable areas in the country and around the world. The camps have approximately 930,000 Rohingya refugees divided into 33 camps. These camps are located near the northern edge of the Bay of Bengal. Refugees&#x2019; shelters are largely constructed from bamboo poles and tarpaulins - making them highly vulnerable to the impacts of cyclones, including flooding and landslides. 
 
Through this project, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) is carrying out site development and site management interventions in response to the damage caused by Tropical Cyclone Mocha in May 2023. These interventions are contributing towards the improvement of living conditions for Rohingya refugees and restoring a healthier environment able to withstand climactic shocks. IOM is also reinforcing its efforts to address the immediate needs of affected Rohingya refugees ahead of the monsoon season this year.</narrative>
  </description>
